      "note": "Private Core control write for one Connection's Worker fence, and deliberately absent from every advertised surface: it is in no manifest tag and in no MCP tool list, because which process may execute is not a model decision and a seventh tool would put a self-serving generation one prompt away. Authorized by the Board owner/service principal key and refused outright when the request carries a Connection identity, since the Connection is the thing being fenced. workerId is required and may be explicit null to withdraw the holder; an omitted workerId is a shape error, never a withdrawal. generation is an exact integer in 1..=9007199254740991. Every holder change, withdrawal included, needs a strictly higher generation; the same generation is idempotent only for the identical holder, so a lost acknowledgement is resent under the same requestId and costs no generation. On acceptance the payload carries connectionId, the fence in force under fence as {workerId, generation}, and replay true when the fence already stood at that value; a caller must read the returned fence rather than echo its own request. On refusal the errorCode is fence_generation_stale and the payload carries connectionId and currentFence, the unchanged fence, so a carrier reconciles upward from the authoritative floor rather than counting from its own memory across a restart or a secret rotation. Core stores the holder and generation only: lease expiry is the carrier's judgement and no expiry is copied here. Withdrawal (workerId:null) remains permitted during Board suspension or exhausted Case deadlines because it only narrows execution rights; this exemption does not admit a new holder."
