      "note": "PROPOSED (PSC 0.2.5): the product owner decided on 2026-09-30 that an original call that can be neither dispatched nor resolved needs a manual exit before the release; this text awaits the owner's review. Private Core governance write for an operator's manual withdrawal of one accepted invocation that was never dispatched. Authorization is the same as ResolveInvocation: current Board write permission, governance role and no Connection or Worker identity; a Gateway sends it only after checking that the Agent belongs to the signed-in account and the operator has confirmed the withdrawal of that exact pending call in the Console. It names only the invocation and its immutable ApplyAction receipt key. Unlike ResolveInvocation the verdict is the operator's, so Core verifies only the conditions under which a withdrawal can hold: the exact original acceptance and frozen contract, a retained original fence proving that no claim, report or unknown disposition was accepted, no trusted host dispatch, effect, unknown or receipt record, and the awaiting_dispatch phase. Refused with already_dispatched when any execution is recorded; the outcome then comes from the Worker report or from ReconcileUnknownExecution. Incomplete or inconsistent history, such as an invocation accepted before fences were kept, is refused with invocation_history_unconfirmed and withdraws nothing. On success Core writes host invocation_refused under the identity ResolveInvocation uses, so an invocation has at most one refusal, with the fixed reason \"Withdrawn by the owner before dispatch. This invocation was never dispatched to a Worker, so it had no external effect; the Action may be requested again.\"; the invocation becomes refused, its pending-execution fence retires, a later ClaimWork is refused with not_dispatchable, and the original call returns that refusal to the model. ClaimWork and this command share the Board commit boundary, so exactly one of them takes effect. It executes nothing and only narrows execution, like FenceWorker withdrawing a fence holder (workerId null), so it does not consult the original caller's current authority, preconditions, policy, Case pause, Board suspension (SetBoardSuspended) or the state in which every live Case is past its deadline (other writes then get board_draining): during suspension ReconcileUnknownExecution is refused and ResolveInvocation answers waiting, while a withdrawal still applies; with every live Case past its deadline it applies, as ReconcileUnknownExecution does. On an invocation that is already refused it writes nothing, creates no Board generation and answers the recorded refusal reason with replay true. Original accepted replies remain immutable. The Java Core INTERNAL projection reports the refusal as invocationRefusalKind invalidated (a human veto is human); that kind is an implementation projection field, not defined by this protocol. It is not automatic recovery: nothing issues it without an operator's confirmation. This operation is never advertised to models or Workers."
