      "note": "Private Core control write recording one immutable unknown disposition for an invocation whose Worker was permanently lost (approved contract SYS-FUNC-04 / ACC-ACT-05). Like FenceWorker it is in no manifest tag and in no MCP tool list: whether an execution counts as unresolved is an operator decision, not a model one. It names only the invocation and the operator's stated reason. Everything the disposition rests on is read from state the caller does not own: the trusted dispatch record supplies the Connection and the Worker instance that took the work, and the Worker fence in force supplies the generation that proves that instance can no longer claim or report. A caller cannot assert that the old Worker is isolated and cannot name a generation. Authorization is the intersection of two configured surfaces: the deployment-level governance role for the authenticated principal (BOARDD_PRINCIPAL_ROLES or its _FILE form, a server-owned table with no Board dimension; roles are never taken from a request header and never implied by possessing a key), and write access to this Board under the Board ACL (BOARDD_BOARD_ACL), which boardd requires on any non-loopback bind. A loopback-only bind does not force an ACL, so Board-level narrowing there is a deployment premise rather than something Core supplies. Refused outright when the request carries a Connection identity, since the Connection is the one being reconciled. Refused with worker_not_isolated when the fence has not advanced past the recorded instance, with not_dispatchable when no trusted dispatch record names the invocation, and with already_reported when a trusted effect_confirmed or effect_failed already settled it. Recording is idempotent per invocation: a second request writes nothing, bumps nothing, and replays the original record, so a retry duplicates neither the record nor its cost. The record does not certify success, does not satisfy any acceptance or business effect condition, and closes no Case; the outcome stays unknown. It releases three gates for this invocation and nothing else. Two are Case gates of its attributed roots (Case Context section 5): it stops blocking a non-completed CloseCase, and it stops counting against PauseCase admission, where neither the host frontier nor the execution obligations count it (every other pause condition still applies; for example, a Board whose live roots have all expired still refuses PauseCase with board_draining). The third is Board-wide, so it also covers a Board-level invocation that has no attributed root: it stops counting against the quiescence check of an explicit whole-Board program migration (UpdateBoardProgram mode migrate), and when that migration activates, its reply lists the carried invocations in reconciledUnknownInvocations. Drain activation still requires every live root to be terminal. A completed CloseCase and a completed SealBoard still count the invocation, and reconciliation releases no other obligation: verification, claimable action, review and evidence (material) obligations count exactly as they would without it. The pause and migration refinements are PSC 0.2.5 proposals adopted by the Java Core; the retiring Rust Core releases only the non-completed CloseCase gate."
